1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,300
The Hills Singapore Dug Into the Sea

2
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:08,924
In the 1880s, three hills stood along
the waterfront south of Singapore's town:

3
00:00:09,004 --> 00:00:12,325
Mount Erskine, Mount
Wallich and Mount Palmer.

4
00:00:12,325 --> 00:00:15,782
Within fifty years, Mount
Wallich and most of Mount

5
00:00:15,862 --> 00:00:19,319
Palmer had been blasted,
dug up and carted into the

6
00:00:19,399 --> 00:00:23,075
sea to make new land, and
Mount Erskine has gone too.

7
00:00:23,075 --> 00:00:26,058
Today the only reminders are a few street

8
00:00:26,138 --> 00:00:29,650
names and one small knoll
with a shrine on top.

9
00:00:29,650 --> 00:00:32,175
Singapore is not flat.

10
00:00:32,175 --> 00:00:36,548
Its highest point, Bukit Timah
Hill, rises to 164 metres,

11
00:00:36,628 --> 00:00:39,439
and ridges run from the centre of the

12
00:00:39,519 --> 00:00:43,425
island down to Mount Faber
and the southern coast.

13
00:00:43,425 --> 00:00:46,477
In the early town, the
waterfront to the south

14
00:00:46,557 --> 00:00:49,825
was hilly too, but most
of those hills are gone.

15
00:00:49,825 --> 00:00:52,712
They sat close to the
sea at a time when the

16
00:00:52,792 --> 00:00:56,353
colony needed earth to fill
it, and they stood between

17
00:00:56,433 --> 00:00:59,400
the town and the new
docks at Tanjong Pagar.

18
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:02,875
The smallest story
belongs to Mount Wallich.

19
00:01:02,875 --> 00:01:06,188
In 1822 the Danish
botanist Nathaniel Wallich,

20
00:01:06,268 --> 00:01:10,909
the superintendent of the Calcutta
botanic garden, fell ill on a

21
00:01:10,989 --> 00:01:15,113
visit to Singapore and spent
about five months recovering

22
00:01:15,193 --> 00:01:19,250
in a house called Botany Hall
on a hill near the shore.

23
00:01:19,250 --> 00:01:22,656
During his stay Stamford
Raffles persuaded him to

24
00:01:22,736 --> 00:01:26,996
propose a botanic and experimental
garden for the settlement,

25
00:01:27,076 --> 00:01:29,850
and the hill was later named after him.

26
00:01:29,850 --> 00:01:34,725
Mount Erskine stood to the north
of it, behind Telok Ayer Street.

27
00:01:34,725 --> 00:01:39,232
It lasted longer than its
neighbour: the Straits Times of the

28
00:01:39,312 --> 00:01:44,200
25th of August 1913 reported that
the government had acquired it.

29
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:49,325
Beside it was Ann Siang Hill, which
still survives as a small park.

30
00:01:49,325 --> 00:01:53,246
Mount Palmer was the largest of the three,

31
00:01:53,326 --> 00:01:56,375
about 119 feet (36 metres) high.

32
00:01:56,375 --> 00:02:00,647
It was named after John
Palmer, a Calcutta merchant who

33
00:02:00,727 --> 00:02:05,475
bought the hill soon after 1819
and built a bungalow on top.

34
00:02:05,475 --> 00:02:09,296
When his business ran into
trouble, he sold part of

35
00:02:09,376 --> 00:02:12,967
it in 1828 to a Parsi,
and its slopes became the

36
00:02:13,047 --> 00:02:16,409
burial ground of
Singapore's Parsi community,

37
00:02:16,489 --> 00:02:20,925
so the hill was also known as
Mount Parsee or Bukit Parsi.

38
00:02:20,925 --> 00:02:26,328
Part of it overlooking the harbour
was fortified as Fort Palmer,

39
00:02:26,408 --> 00:02:29,750
which by 1864 had five 56-pounder guns.

40
00:02:29,750 --> 00:02:32,520
Its later owners included Hoo Ah Kay,

41
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:35,450
the merchant better known as Whampoa.

42
00:02:35,450 --> 00:02:39,875
The Telok Ayer reclamation,
begun in 1879, needed earth,

43
00:02:39,955 --> 00:02:44,300
and the nearest earth was
in the hills behind the bay.

44
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:48,919
On a Thursday evening in November
1884, according to the Straits

45
00:02:48,999 --> 00:02:53,545
Times Weekly Issue, a charge of
1,000 pounds of gunpowder and a

46
00:02:53,625 --> 00:02:58,171
few pounds of dynamite was fired
in a 50-foot tunnel bored into

47
00:02:58,251 --> 00:03:02,950
the face of Mount Wallich, beside
the new road to Tanjong Pagar.

48
00:03:02,950 --> 00:03:06,714
The explosion made the ground
tremble for some distance,

49
00:03:06,794 --> 00:03:11,383
and the paper reported that about
50,000 tons of rock and earth were

50
00:03:11,463 --> 00:03:15,925
brought down in one blast, broken
small enough to be carted away.

51
00:03:15,925 --> 00:03:19,312
The hill was taken down
over the following years,

52
00:03:19,392 --> 00:03:23,425
and its earth went into the
new land behind Raffles Quay.

53
00:03:23,425 --> 00:03:25,932
Wallich Street, named in 1899,

54
00:03:26,012 --> 00:03:30,150
runs across the site of
the vanished hill today.

55
00:03:30,150 --> 00:03:33,125
The hills were also in the way.

56
00:03:33,125 --> 00:03:37,244
Goods from the docks at Tanjong
Pagar had to be carried by

57
00:03:37,324 --> 00:03:41,877
bullock cart over or around them
to reach the town, and from the

58
00:03:41,957 --> 00:03:46,300
early 1870s the removal of Mount
Palmer was being suggested.

59
00:03:46,300 --> 00:03:51,026
In 1905 Fort Palmer, the last
military post in Tanjong Pagar,

60
00:03:51,106 --> 00:03:55,201
was demolished, and the hill
was cut down to fill the

61
00:03:55,281 --> 00:03:59,850
Telok Ayer Basin, the second,
larger reclamation offshore.

62
00:03:59,850 --> 00:04:05,706
By the time the basin was completed in 1932,
most of Mount Palmer had been levelled,

63
00:04:05,786 --> 00:04:09,169
except for a small knoll
that held the remains of

64
00:04:09,249 --> 00:04:14,125
the Parsi burial ground and the back
of the Fook Tet Soo Khek temple.

65
00:04:14,125 --> 00:04:18,744
The road laid out across the site
was named Palmer Battery Road,

66
00:04:18,824 --> 00:04:21,100
later shortened to Palmer Road.

67
00:04:21,100 --> 00:04:25,711
On that last knoll stands the
Keramat Habib Noh, the shrine of

68
00:04:25,791 --> 00:04:30,403
a 19th-century Muslim saint,
Habib Noh bin Mohammed al-Habshi,

69
00:04:30,483 --> 00:04:35,250
with the Masjid Haji Muhammad
Salleh, built in 1903, beside it.

70
00:04:35,250 --> 00:04:39,196
In 2016, when the Prince Edward
MRT station was announced,

71
00:04:39,276 --> 00:04:42,181
what remained of the
foothills made way for

72
00:04:42,261 --> 00:04:45,721
the new station and bus
terminal, while the shrine,

73
00:04:45,801 --> 00:04:49,550
the mosque and the Fook Tet
Soo Khek temple were kept.

74
00:04:49,550 --> 00:04:52,418
The other names are all on street signs:

75
00:04:52,498 --> 00:04:56,700
Wallich Street, Erskine Road,
Palmer Road and Parsi Road.

76
00:04:56,700 --> 00:04:59,726
Ann Siang Hill, the one
hill of the group that

77
00:04:59,806 --> 00:05:03,507
was not cut down, is now a
small park above Club Street,

78
00:05:03,587 --> 00:05:05,950
with a boardwalk through the trees.

79
00:05:05,950 --> 00:05:10,050
The largest cutting of hills
came after independence.

80
00:05:10,050 --> 00:05:14,560
For the East Coast reclamation,
which began in 1966 and added

81
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:19,527
1,525 hectares of land from Bedok
to Tanjong Rhu, hills at Siglap,

82
00:05:19,607 --> 00:05:24,042
Bedok and Tampines were cut
away by bucket-wheel excavators,

83
00:05:24,122 --> 00:05:28,332
and conveyor belts carried
the earth to a jetty at Bedok,

84
00:05:28,412 --> 00:05:32,400
where it was loaded onto
barges and taken out to sea.

85
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:34,870
Where it fits in the bigger story:

86
00:05:34,950 --> 00:05:39,900
Singapore's reclamation is usually
told as land made from the sea.

87
00:05:39,900 --> 00:05:43,850
Much of that land was first
a hill somewhere else.

88
00:05:43,850 --> 00:05:46,937
The waterfront south of
the old town lost three

89
00:05:47,017 --> 00:05:50,238
hills this way, and their
names on today's street

90
00:05:50,318 --> 00:05:53,350
signs are the only sign
they were ever there.
